---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import PasswordGenerator from '../../components/PasswordGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Password Generator" description="Generate strong, random passwords with custom length and character rules — cryptographically secure, no signup.">
  <PasswordGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why randomness matters</h2>
  <p>A strong password isn't just long — it needs to be unpredictable. This generator uses your browser's cryptographically secure random number generator (<code>crypto.getRandomValues</code>), not a weaker pseudo-random function like <code>Math.random()</code>, to produce passwords that resist both brute-force and pattern-based guessing.</p>

  <h2>Features</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Adjustable length from short PINs to long passphrases</li>
    <li>Uses your browser's cryptographically secure random source, not a weak PRNG</li>
    <li>One-click copy to clipboard</li>
    <li>Runs entirely client-side — nothing is transmitted or logged</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Set your desired password length using the slider.</li>
    <li>The password updates instantly as you adjust it.</li>
    <li>Click the generated password to copy it, or click "Regenerate" for a new one.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>A 16-character generated password might look like <code>xT9!qL2vB@k7Rw4Z</code> — a mix of uppercase, lowercase, numbers, and symbols with no dictionary pattern.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reusing the same password across sites.</strong> Even a strong password loses its value if one breach exposes it everywhere else you used it.</li>
    <li><strong>Storing passwords in plain text</strong> (a notes app, a spreadsheet). Use a password manager instead.</li>
    <li><strong>Choosing length over unpredictability.</strong> A long password built from a predictable pattern (e.g. repeating characters) is weaker than it looks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Generating a new account password you'll store in a password manager</li>
    <li>Creating temporary/throwaway credentials for testing</li>
    <li>Setting a strong default password for a new server or database user</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is this safe to use for real accounts?</strong> Yes — generation happens locally in your browser and nothing is transmitted or logged.</p>
  <p><strong>Why not just use a memorable phrase?</strong> Memorable phrases are easier for humans to guess or crack via dictionary attacks than truly random character strings of the same length.</p>
</ToolLayout>
